      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_777615fc7b140331d25a245c47a86ae4cb33c6b4cd0c1a7a9d8b057dd9b3be28",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: in explicitly informal comments, Lehmer interprets an exceptionally low maximum as delicate cancellation between early rising and later descending terms, invokes Kronecker's theorem for recurrence of this phenomenon, and proposes searches in selected remote neighborhoods; a note reports 15,000 additional on-line roots. Interpretation: the term decomposition turns a local near-cancellation into a heuristic for targeting future stress regions rather than merely extending a contiguous table. Recurrence concerns the cancellation phenomenon, not RH counterexamples, and neither the heuristic nor the extra finite computation licenses a global conclusion. OCR-obscured sign language is not sharpened.",
